# No ANSI / VT100 for Windows shells (hard rule)

Operator directive (Phil, 2026-08): classic Windows shells do **not** reliably
render ANSI / VT100 escape sequences. Emitting `\x1b[` styling into `cmd.exe` or
Windows PowerShell produces garbage in operator logs, installer transcripts, and
redirected captures. Color is optional on Unix terminals only, and **plain text
is preferred**.

## The rule

> **Do not generate ANSI / VT100 escape sequences when the target is Windows
> Command Prompt or Windows PowerShell.** That may be OK on Linux/macOS
> terminals that support it, but it does **not** work reliably in classic
> Windows shells. On Linux/macOS, ANSI must be **configurable**, with **plain
> text preferred** as the default (or easy opt-in for color).

1. **Windows (`cmd.exe`, Windows PowerShell 5.x, redirected hosts):** never
   write CSI / OSC / VT100 sequences (`\x1b[…`, `\033[…`, raw
   `$esc = [char]27` strings) to stdout or stderr for operator-facing CLIs,
   installers, services, or scripts.
2. **Preferred output:** plain US English text; structured fields (`tracing`
   key=value); status words like `[ OK ]` / `FAIL` that do not rely on color
   alone (Section 508 / WCAG 2.0 AA 1.4.1).
3. **Linux/macOS:** color is opt-in. Manifest / CLI default is `plain`. `auto`
   may colorize only when the stream is an interactive TTY **and** industry
   disable switches are unset.
4. **Do not invent AIC product env vars** for color (see
   [`no-environment-variables.mdc`](no-environment-variables.mdc)). Product CLIs
   use manifest keys (`LOG_FORMAT` / `log_format`) and/or CLI flags (`--color` /
   `--no-color`). `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR=0` are **industry terminal
   conventions** already honored by many deps — reading them inside a dedicated
   terminal-styling helper is an allowed exception; do not document them as AIC
   configuration.

## Forbidden constructs

In shipping CLIs, installers, PowerShell under `pim-installers` / `scripts/`,
Rust `println!` paths, and Node tooling that targets Windows operators:

- Emitting `\x1b[`, `\u001b[`, `\033[`, or `$([char]27) + '['` style strings to
  stdout/stderr for Windows consumers.
- Unconditional use of `owo-colors`, `colored`, `console::style`, `yansi`,
  `nu-ansi-term`, `clap` color, Node `chalk` / `kleur` / `ansi-styles` when the
  binary or script runs under classic Windows shells without a plain-text gate.
- `tracing_subscriber` / `env_logger` `.with_ansi(true)` or
  `WriteStyle::Always` on Windows under `auto` / default settings.
- PowerShell that builds ANSI strings for status:
  `$esc = [char]27; Write-Output "$esc[32mOK$esc[0m"`.
- Relying on color alone to convey success/failure (Section 508).

## Preferred patterns

| Surface | Do this |
| --- | --- |
| Rust operator CLI / daemon stderr | Plain by default. Gate via `LOG_FORMAT` / `log_format` = `plain` \| `auto` \| `ansi`. On **Windows**, `auto` **never** enables ANSI. On Unix, `auto` only when the stream `is_terminal()`. |
| Color crates | Prefer `anstyle` + explicit false on `cfg(windows)` unless ConPTY / VT is proven; or strip with a shared `strip_ansi_escapes` helper before Win32/Tauri panes. |
| PowerShell | `Write-Host -ForegroundColor Green` (native console API — OK) **or** plain `Write-Output`. Never raw ESC sequences. |
| Node (Windows operator tools) | Disable chalk/kleur by default on `win32`; require `--color` for opt-in. |
| Captured / GUI panes | Always strip ANSI before display (`pim_app_config_cli::strip_ansi_escapes`). |

Canonical Rust ladder (`tracing_stderr_ansi.rs`, server + agent): `NO_COLOR` /
`CLICOLOR=0` → plain; then `LOG_FORMAT` / `log_format` (`plain` | `auto` |
`ansi`); `auto` + Windows → plain always; `auto` + Unix → color only if
`stream.is_terminal()`. Defaults in `app-config.toml` are
`default_dev = "plain"`, `default_prod = "plain"`.

## Worked violation → fix

**Violation:**

```rust
use owo_colors::OwoColorize;
println!("{} service started", "ok".green());
```

On Windows PowerShell this often prints literal `←[32mok←[0m service started`.

**Fix** — plain by default; never colorize on Windows for operator CLIs:

```rust
#[cfg(windows)]
fn paint_ok(s: &str) -> String { s.to_string() }

#[cfg(not(windows))]
fn paint_ok(s: &str) -> String {
    if stderr_use_ansi() {
        use owo_colors::OwoColorize;
        format!("{}", s.green())
    } else {
        s.to_string()
    }
}

println!("{} service started", paint_ok("[ OK ]"));
```

PowerShell parallel: replace `$esc = [char]27; Write-Host "$esc[31mFAIL$esc[0m"`
with `Write-Host "FAIL endpoint missing" -ForegroundColor Red` or plain
`Write-Output`.

## Industry env exceptions (narrow)

`NO_COLOR` (any non-empty value, [no-color.org](https://no-color.org/)) and
`CLICOLOR=0` force plain. Both are **terminal conventions, not AIC product
configuration**. Do not add `$env:AIC_COLOR`, `OFFLINE_LOG_COLOR`, or similar,
and do not instruct operators to set env vars for AIC settings — use
`pim-app-config` / configurator / `--color` flags. Allowed read sites: the
dedicated `tracing_stderr_ansi` (or equivalent) helper only; keep
`forbid_env_var_reads` allowlists in sync.

